    "result_text": "The full test suite (the single `AcceptanceTest`, the only test class in the project) passes with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation cap in the Spring MVC create-owner flow, following the existing pre-persistence rejection-rule pattern.\n\n**New exception** \u2014 `rest/advice/DailyOwnerLimitExceededException.java`: a `RuntimeException` mirroring the existing `CityCapacityExceededException`.\n\n**Exception handler** \u2014 `rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`: added `handleDailyOwnerLimitExceededException` returning `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` (429) as a `ProblemDetail`, plus its error-message constant.\n\n**Controller** \u2014 `rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:\n- Added `MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY = 100`.\n- Added the check as the first rule in the existing `rejectDisallowedOwnerCreation(...)` method, which runs after `normalizeOwnerFields` (so registration dates are already defaulted to today) and before any derived fields or persistence.\n- Added a `countOwnersRegisteredToday()` helper that counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals `LocalDate.now()`; when that count is `>= 100`, it throws the new exception.\n\nThe seed owners carry a null `registration_date`, so they don't count toward the daily total \u2014 the acceptance test creates exactly 100 owners dated today, and the 101st is correctly rejected with 429.",
